  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet and dry conditions through Wednesday afternoon.\n\nMainly quiet weather for the rest of today and tomorrow. This \nafternoon, a weak cold front pushes into the region from the \nnortheast. There is little to no moisture to work with, so expect a \ndry frontal passage with a mixture of clouds. As for Wednesday, the \nfront drifts back to the northeast as a warm front, with southerly \nwinds advecting moisture back into southern New England and PWATs \nrising to 1.4 to 1.6 inches. While we cannot rule out a WAA shower \nduring the day, the general theme is for a drier day with increased \ncloud cover. Otherwise, a seasonably warm day with highs in the \nupper 70s and lower 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frontal passage Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning brings showers and gusty southwest winds, especially over \nfar southeastern Massachusetts, Cape Cod, and the Islands.\n\nShower chances increase Wednesday night into Thursday morning \nassociated with a cold front. Though this doesn't look to be a \nsoaking event, it will be more showery in nature, where one town \nsees a heavier downpour while two towns over perhaps only a light \nrain. More impressive are the low-level winds. Depending on your \nflavor of guidance, 925 mb winds are 45 to 55 knots. However, the \nstrongest winds aloft traverse southern New England predawn \nThursday, which will limit the amount of wind that can mix down to \nthe surface.\n\nThinking where overnight temperatures remain mild, across far \nsoutheastern Massachusetts, Cape Cod, and the Islands, there is the \ngreatest chance for winds to mix down, with sub-advisory gusts of 30 \nto 40 mph. However, if temperatures were to be a few degrees warmer \novernight in this area, this could allow for slightly deeper mixing. \nThough with the nocturnal inversion overhead, it will be difficult \nto realize those stronger winds aloft and transport them down to the \nsurface. Nevertheless, it's something to monitor.\n\nCurrently, we feel the greatest impacts will be for the marine \ncommunity. Around the Cape and Islands are Gale Watches for the \npotential of those stronger winds. By Thursday afternoon, the \nsurface cold front is offshore. However, temperatures aloft are \nslower to lower, so it will still be fairly seasonable Thursday \nafternoon. Then comes a reinforcement of colder air Thursday night, \nwith 925 mb temperatures falling from around +20C Thursday to +10C \nFriday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Full sun, seasonable temperatures and onshore \nbreezes Friday into the weekend.\n\nStill looking at a really nice stretch of weather for Friday into \nmuch of the weekend, with the weather across Southern New England \nbeing dominated by a sprawling high pressure cell coming in from \nCanada. Expect full sun, low humidity levels and dry weather for the \ndaytime hours with good radiational cooling supporting some cool \nnights Friday through Saturday night. It still looks like Sunday \ncould offer the next chance for cloud cover and some light showers \ndeveloping Sunday night into early Monday but these also don't look \ncapable of washouts. Highs Fri and Sat into the mid to upper 70s \nwith low humidity levels, except low 70s near the eastern \ncoastlines. By Sunday highs should top out in the mid 70s to low 80s \ndespite increasing cloud cover.",
